    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/52781": {
      "title": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Postdoctoral Researcher - Lyme Disease Bacteriophage Biology",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Postdoctoral Researcher - Lyme Disease Bacteriophage Biology Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Postdoctoral Researcher - Lyme Disease Bacteriophage Biology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is not eligible for new sponsorship. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number STAFF - VA - 27075 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Patrick Secor patrick.secor@montana.edu 406-994-6046 Classification Title Postdoctoral Researcher Working Title Postdoctoral Researcher - Lyme Disease Bacteriophage Biology Brief Position Overview The Secor Lab seeks a postdoctoral researcher to investigate bacteriophage biology in the Lyme disease spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi. The successful candidate will help define how phiBB-1 controls horizontal gene transfer and bacterial adaptation across the tick-mouse infectious cycle. Position Number 4C2248 Department Microbiology & Cell Biology Division College of Agriculture/MAES Appointment Type Research Professional Contract Term Fiscal Year Semester If other, specify From date If other, specify End date FLSA Exempt Union Affiliation Exempt from Collective Bargaining FTE 1.0 Benefits Eligible Eligible Salary Salary commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications. Contract Type LOA If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement The Department of Microbiology & Cell Biology at Montana State University invites applications for a full-time, benefits-eligible Postdoctoral Researcher in the Secor Laboratory. The Secor Lab studies how bacteria, bacteriophages, and animal hosts interact to shape microbial evolution and disease. This NIH / NIAID -funded project centers on the temperate bacteriophage phiBB-1, the cp32 prophage family, and phage-mediated movement of antigen and other genetic material among B. burgdorferi strains. The project combines mechanistic phage biology with the natural tick-vector and vertebrate-host cycle. A candidate will develop a research focus that builds on their strengths; prior Lyme disease or tick experience is welcome but is not required. Duties and Responsibilities Design and conduct independent and collaborative studies of phiBB-1/cp32 biology, phage replication, lysis-lysogeny regulation, transduction, and horizontal gene transfer. Investigate how phage activity and genetic exchange influence B. burgdorferi across tick and mammalian host environments. Use and/or develop approaches in bacterial or phage genetics, molecular biology, vector biology, animal infection, microscopy, genomics, transcriptomics, biochemistry, or computational analysis, depending on expertise. Analyze data, maintain reproducible records, present findings, and lead or contribute to publications and funding applications. Collaborate across disciplines and contribute to mentoring and a safe, constructive research environment. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills PhD or equivalent doctoral degree completed by the start date in microbiology, bacteriology, molecular biology, genetics or genomics, vector biology or entomology, infectious disease, immunology, biochemistry, bioinformatics or computational biology, or a closely related field. Research experience in at least one relevant area: bacterial or phage genetics; molecular biology or biochemistry; arthropod vector biology; animal models of infection; microscopy; genomics or transcriptomics; or quantitative/computational biology. Evidence of research productivity through a peer-reviewed publication, preprint, or substantive dissertation research. Experience analyzing research data and documenting experimental or computational methods and results. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Experience in one or more of the following: bacteriophages or prophages; transduction or horizontal gene transfer; Borrelia or other spirochetes; ticks or other arthropod vectors; mouse models of infection; bacterial genetic manipulation; fluorescence or electron microscopy; genome sequencing, RNA -seq, or bioinformatics. Experience developing, optimizing, or troubleshooting complex experimental or computational workflows. Experience working in a collaborative or multidisciplinary research setting. Experience mentoring or training students or research staff. The Successful Candidate Will Demonstrate intellectual curiosity and the ability to connect questions across microbiology, phage biology, vector biology, and host-pathogen biology. Plan and advance research with appropriate independence while seeking input when useful. Communicate clearly, collaborate constructively, and contribute to an inclusive and supportive research environment. Approach experimental challenges with persistence, sound judgment, and attention to rigor and reproducibility. Position Special Requirements/Additional Information The position requires completion of applicable biosafety, animal-use, and arthropod-containment training. This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Physical Demands Work may include extended periods at a laboratory bench or computer; use of standard laboratory instruments and personal protective equipment; fine motor tasks; movement of materials weighing up to 25 pounds; and occasional work in animal or arthropod research spaces. Some experiments may require occasional evening or weekend time. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodations. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. This position has supervisory duties? No Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Screening of applications will begin on September 15, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Special Instructions This position is not eligible for new sponsorship. EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
